        # "How to read" guides.
        # ES: Guías "Cómo leer".
        guide = list(
            mga = .("A multigroup analysis asks whether the relationships of the model differ between groups, for example between men and women or between two types of customers. It has two parts. First, MICOM (Henseler, Ringle & Sarstedt, 2016, see references) checks that each construct means the same in every group: comparing paths between groups whose constructs are built differently compares different things. Then the paths are compared, pair of groups by pair of groups."),
            micom = .("MICOM has three steps. Step 1, configural invariance: the same indicators, data treatment and algorithm in every group; the module guarantees it. Step 2, compositional invariance: the weights of each group build the same composite. c is the correlation between the scores computed with the weights of one group and those computed with the weights of the other; if c is not significantly below 1 (permutation p of .05 or more), step 2 holds. Step 3: equal means and equal variances of the composites in both groups, also tested by permutation. Reading: if step 2 fails, the construct cannot be compared (no invariance); if step 2 holds, its paths can be compared (partial invariance); if step 3 also holds, the groups could be pooled and analyzed together (full invariance). A non-significant result means that no difference was detected, not that the groups are proven equal."),
            mgd = .("The two tests of path differences answer the same question in different ways. The permutation test (Chin & Dibbern, 2010) reassigns the cases to the groups at random many times; the difference is significant when chance alone rarely produces one as large. PLS-MGA (Henseler et al., 2009; Sarstedt et al., 2011) compares the bootstrap distributions of the path in each group. cSEM gives the probability that the path of the first group is not larger than that of the second, a one-sided value: near 0 the first group's path is larger, near 1 the second's, around 0.5 there is no difference. The table turns it into a two-sided p, comparable with that of the permutation test. When both tests agree, the conclusion is firmer; when only one is significant, report both and treat the difference as tentative."),
            mga_cautions = .("Cautions. Each group must, on its own, have enough cases for the model: with small groups only large differences reach significance, so a non-significant difference is not evidence that the paths are equal. Comparing many paths and pairs of groups multiplies the chance of a false positive: use the correction of the Multigroup Analysis box if the comparisons are confirmatory, or present them as exploratory. The groups are observed, not assigned at random: a difference describes the groups, and does not show that belonging to a group causes it. Groups defined by an observed variable do not rule out other, unobserved differences between the cases (unobserved heterogeneity), which this module does not test."),
            hoc = .("A higher-order construct is measured by other constructs, its dimensions (for example, psychological capital by hope, efficacy, resilience and optimism). In the disjoint two-stage approach, stage 1 estimates the dimensions as ordinary constructs, linked to the constructs the higher-order construct is related to, and stage 2 treats their scores as the indicators of the higher-order construct, while the other constructs keep their own indicators (Sarstedt et al., 2019, see references). Read this table as a measurement table one level up: for a reflective higher-order construct, the loadings of its dimensions, its reliability and its AVE, judged on the usual thresholds; for a formative one, the weights of its dimensions, their significance and their VIF. The dimensions themselves are assessed in the first-order tables. Common misreading: drawing structural paths from the dimensions as well as from the higher-order construct; the dimensions only reach the rest of the model through it."),
            hoc_htmt = .("The HTMT table of the higher-order constructs checks each reflective higher-order construct against the other reflective constructs of the model, in stage 2, where it is measured by the scores of its dimensions (Sarstedt et al., 2019, see references). It is never compared with its own dimensions, which correlate highly by design. The thresholds are the usual ones (0.85; 0.90 for conceptually similar constructs), and with the bootstrap the test is the upper bound."),
            fit = .("The overall fit test asks whether the correlations among the indicators that the model implies match the observed ones exactly, apart from sampling error (Dijkstra & Henseler, 2015; Schuberth et al., 2023, see references). Four discrepancies measure the distance between the two matrices: SRMR (standardized root mean squared residual), d_G (geodesic distance), d_L (unweighted least squares distance, called d_ULS in SmartPLS) and d_ML (maximum likelihood distance). The bootstrap first transforms the data so that the model fits them exactly, then resamples them; the critical value of each discrepancy is a high quantile of its resampled values (HI95 in SmartPLS at 95%). If the observed value is above it, exact fit is rejected at that level; p (bootstrap) is, roughly, the share of resamples at least as large as the observed value. Read it with care: not rejecting does not prove the model right, and with a large sample small, unimportant misfits lead to rejection. The SRMR below 0.08 is a descriptive rule of thumb, not a test (Henseler et al., 2016). In PLS-SEM this test is a complement: the main evidence remains the measurement and structural criteria of the tables above (Hair et al., 2019)."),
            fit_saturated = .("Two versions of the model are tested. The estimated model imposes your structural paths; the saturated model lets every pair of constructs correlate freely, so only the measurement model is tested. If the saturated model is not rejected and the estimated one is, the misfit lies in the structural model (for example, a missing path); if both are rejected, look first at the measurement model (indicators that belong to another construct, correlated errors not modeled)."),
            ipma = .("The importance-performance map (IPMA) adds a second dimension to the path coefficients (Ringle & Sarstedt, 2016, see references). For each target construct it places every construct that affects it on two axes: its importance, the unstandardized total effect on the target (direct plus indirect), and its performance, the average of its scores rescaled to 0-100. Performance only means something if all the indicators share one scale, with high values meaning better, and if the range of that scale is right; set it by hand when the data do not reach the ends of the scale."),
            ipma_reading = .("Reading: the dashed lines are the means of importance and performance among the constructs shown. Constructs in the lower right (important, low performance) are where an improvement would raise the target most: improve them first. Upper right: keep up. Upper left: performance is high but matters little for the target (possible overkill). Lower left: low priority. The map is descriptive and rests on the model being right; it says where to look, not what to do."),
            nca = .("Necessary condition analysis (NCA; Dul, 2016, see references) asks a different question from the path coefficients: not whether more of a construct gives more of the outcome on average, but whether a high outcome is possible without a certain level of the construct. If it is not, the construct is a necessary condition: its absence cannot be compensated by the other constructs. Combined with PLS-SEM (Richter et al., 2020, see references), it works on the construct scores of the IPMA (0-100) of every construct with a direct path to each outcome."),
            nca_reading = .("Reading: the ceiling line separates the area with cases from an empty upper-left zone. d is the size of that empty zone relative to the scope; d \u2265 0.1 with a permutation p < .05 indicates a necessary condition. CR-FDH (a straight line) suits continuous scores; CE-FDH (a step function) suits scores with few distinct values. The verdict uses the ceiling chosen in the panel; both are shown."),
            nca_bottleneck = .("The bottleneck table says, for each level of the outcome, the minimum level of each condition needed to reach it. NN means the condition is not necessary at that level."),
            nca_combined = .("The combined table crosses both logics: a must-have is necessary (NCA), a should-have raises the outcome on average (significant path). A construct can be one, both or neither, and each calls for a different action: secure the minimum level of a must-have, increase a should-have."),
            compare = .("The model comparison sets the main model against a competing one, the same model without the paths you list in \u201cLeft out of the competing model\u201d (Prediction box), estimated with the same settings. R\u00b2 always favors the model with more paths, so the decision rests on the information criteria, which balance fit against the number of paths: Sharma et al. (2019, 2021, see references) found BIC and GM, computed for each structural equation, the most reliable for choosing among PLS-SEM models. The model with the lower BIC and GM is preferred; when they disagree, or the differences are small, theory decides. For predictive comparison, run CVPAT against the competing model in the same box."),
            mgcfa = .("The multigroup CFA (MGCFA) asks the same question as MICOM from the common-factor side (Vandenberg & Lance, 2000; Putnick & Bornstein, 2016, see references): do the reflective indicators measure their constructs in the same way in every group? Configural invariance: the same indicators load on the same factors; metric: the loadings are equal, so relationships between constructs can be compared; scalar: the intercepts are equal too, so construct means can be compared. MICOM tests the composites that PLS estimates; the MGCFA tests the reading of the reflective constructs as common factors, the one PLSc estimates. When they agree the conclusion is robust; when they disagree, say which reading of the constructs your study takes."),
            predict_metrics = .("RMSE or MAE? Both measure the typical size of the prediction errors in the metric of the indicator. The RMSE (root mean squared error) squares the errors before averaging, so a few large errors weigh a lot; the MAE (mean absolute error) gives every error its own weight. When the errors are roughly symmetric, the RMSE is the usual choice; when they are clearly asymmetric (long tail on one side), the MAE describes the typical error better (Shmueli et al., 2019). The table shows both, the skewness of the PLS errors and the metric used for each indicator."),
            q2_clarify = .("Q²predict is not the Q² that older PLS-SEM texts report. That one, the Stone-Geisser Q², comes from blindfolding: it hides single data points and predicts them with a model estimated on the same cases, so it measures in-sample predictive relevance and mixes explanation with prediction. Q²predict predicts whole cases that were left out of the estimation, which is a true out-of-sample test (Shmueli et al., 2016). Values from the two are not comparable."),
            predict = .("PLSpredict asks how well the model predicts cases it has not seen (Shmueli et al., 2016, 2019, see references). The data are split into k folds; each fold is predicted by a model estimated on the others, and the whole procedure is repeated with new random folds. For every indicator of an endogenous construct, Q²predict compares the prediction errors with those of the naive benchmark that predicts each case by the training mean: above 0, the model predicts better than that mean. RMSE and MAE are the average sizes of the errors (in the metric of the indicator; MAE is less sensitive to a few large errors), shown for PLS and for LM, a linear regression of each indicator on all the indicators of the exogenous constructs. R² describes the sample at hand; PLSpredict says how far the model generalizes."),
            predict_power = .("Reading (Shmueli et al., 2019): first check that Q²predict is above 0 for every indicator. Then compare the RMSE (or the MAE if the prediction errors are very asymmetric) of PLS with that of LM: lower for all indicators, high predictive power; for most or half of them, medium; for a minority, low; for none, the model lacks predictive power. LM is a demanding benchmark: it uses every exogenous indicator without the constraints of the model."),
            cvpat = .("CVPAT turns the comparison into a test (Liengaard et al., 2021; Sharma et al., 2023). For each case it averages the squared prediction errors over the indicators and the repetitions (the loss), and tests with a paired t test whether the average loss of PLS is lower than that of each benchmark: IA (indicator averages, the same naive benchmark as Q²predict) and LM. Beating IA is the minimum for a model that claims predictive relevance; beating LM is strong evidence. The test is reported for all endogenous constructs together and for each one. You can also leave out paths of your model in the Prediction box: CVPAT then compares your model with that competing model, a two-sided test of which one predicts better."),
            mediation = .("A specific indirect effect is the part of the effect of X on Y that travels through one or more mediators: the product of the path coefficients along the chain (for X \u2192 M \u2192 Y, a × b). The direct, indirect and total effects table above sums all chains between two constructs; this table separates them. Products of coefficients are not normally distributed, so their significance comes from the percentile bootstrap interval, not from a t test (Preacher & Hayes, 2008; Nitzl et al., 2016, see references). Run the bootstrap to obtain the intervals and the type of mediation."),
            mediation_types = .("The type of mediation (Zhao et al., 2010) combines the indirect effect with the direct path X \u2192 Y. Complementary: both significant with the same sign; the mediator explains part of the effect. Competitive: both significant with opposite signs; the mediator pushes against the direct effect, and the total effect can hide both. Indirect-only (full mediation): only the indirect effect is significant. Direct-only: only the direct path is significant; there is no mediation through this chain. No effect: neither is significant. The old practice of requiring a significant total effect first (Baron & Kenny) and the VAF ratio are no longer recommended. A full mediation can only be claimed when the direct path X \u2192 Y is in the model; if it is not, the table says so."),
            fit_not_shown = .("Why there is no chi-square test, CFI or RMSEA: the ML chi-square test and the indices built on it (CFI, NFI, NNFI, IFI, RMSEA, GFI) belong to covariance-based SEM, where the model is estimated by maximum likelihood and the statistic follows a chi-square distribution under multivariate normality. PLS estimates are not maximum likelihood estimates, so the statistic does not follow that distribution, and its p-value and the indices derived from it have no known meaning here; the bootstrap test above needs neither normality nor a known distribution (Dijkstra & Henseler, 2015; Schuberth et al., 2023)."),
            moderation = .("An interaction X \u00d7 M asks whether the effect of X on the outcome depends on the moderator M. Its coefficient is the change in that effect when M rises by one standard deviation. The table gives the simple effect of X at three values of M (one standard deviation below the mean, the mean and one above), and the plot draws the same idea twice: the simple slopes (left) and the floodlight analysis (right), the simple effect over the whole observed range of M with its bootstrap interval. The Johnson-Neyman points mark where that interval starts or stops including 0. The first construct of the term is the predictor and the second the moderator, in the order the term was built in the panel. Common misreading: concluding there is moderation because the simple effect is significant at one value of M; moderation is the interaction coefficient itself, and its interval must exclude 0."),
            moderation_f2 = .("The f\u00b2 of an interaction is judged on smaller benchmarks than other paths: 0.005, 0.01 and 0.025 for small, medium and large (Kenny, 2018; Hair et al., 2021, see references), because moderating effects are typically small."),
            model = .("Each row is one construct: how it is measured (reflective or formative), how many indicators it has, and its role in the structural model. Exogenous constructs are only predictors; endogenous constructs are predicted by at least one other construct. Check that this table describes the model you intended before reading any number."),
            measurement = .("Loadings are the correlations between each indicator and its construct; weights are the contribution of each indicator to the construct score. For reflective constructs read the loadings: values of 0.708 or more mean the construct explains at least half of the indicator's variance (Hair et al., 2019, see references). For formative constructs read the weights: they show the relative contribution of each indicator, and a small or negative weight is not by itself a reason to remove an indicator. Common misreading: judging formative indicators by their loadings as if they were reflective."),
            reliability = .("One row per reflective construct. Cronbach's alpha is a lower bound of reliability and composite reliability (\u03c1C) an upper bound; \u03c1A usually lies between them and is the most exact of the three (Hair et al., 2019, see references). Values between 0.70 and 0.95 are satisfactory; 0.60 to 0.70 is acceptable only in exploratory research; above 0.95 the indicators are probably redundant (the same question asked several times). The average variance extracted (AVE) is the share of indicator variance the construct explains on average: 0.50 or more means convergent validity. Common misreading: reading only alpha, which tends to underestimate reliability when loadings differ."),
            reliability_omega = .("\u03c1C is McDonald's \u03c9 computed with the loadings of the PLS model. With PLS these are composite loadings, which are inflated, so \u03c1C is an upper bound of reliability; with consistent PLS they are common-factor loadings and \u03c1C is close to the \u03c9 of a factor analysis. The optional \u03c9 (CFA) column estimates a one-factor confirmatory factor analysis of each construct, outside the PLS model (McDonald, 1999, see references): it is the reference used in psychometrics. Common misreading: reporting \u03c1C from plain PLS as if it were the \u03c9 of a factor analysis."),
            htmt = .("One row per pair of reflective constructs. The heterotrait-monotrait ratio (HTMT) compares the correlations between the indicators of two different constructs with the correlations within each construct (Henseler et al., 2015, see references). Values near 1 mean the two constructs are not distinguished by the data. Below 0.85 the constructs are distinct; up to 0.90 is acceptable when they are conceptually similar (for example, two kinds of satisfaction). HTMT2 uses geometric instead of arithmetic means and is more accurate when loadings differ (Roemer et al., 2021, see references). Common misreading: treating HTMT as a correlation between the constructs; it is a ratio of indicator correlations. A formal test needs the bootstrap (Bootstrap box): discriminant validity holds when the upper bound of HTMT stays below the threshold."),
            vif = .("One row per indicator of a formative construct. The variance inflation factor (VIF) measures how much an indicator overlaps with the other indicators of the same construct. Because formative indicators compete to explain the construct, high overlap makes their weights unstable and can turn them negative. VIF of 5 or more is critical; 3 to 5 may be a problem (Hair et al., 2019, see references). Common misreading: removing an indicator only because its VIF is high; a formative indicator carries part of the content of the construct, so first consider merging indicators or building a higher-order construct."),
            f2 = .("The effect size f\u00b2 tells how much the R\u00b2 of the outcome drops when that predictor is removed: f\u00b2 = (R\u00b2 with \u2212 R\u00b2 without) / (1 \u2212 R\u00b2 with). Values of 0.02, 0.15 and 0.35 are read as small, medium and large (Hair et al., 2019, see references). A significant path can have a negligible f\u00b2 in a large sample: read both."),
            vif_struct = .("The structural VIF measures how much the predictors of the same outcome overlap. Above 3, and certainly above 5, their coefficients become unstable and can even change sign (Hair et al., 2019, see references). With a single predictor there is no collinearity to assess."),
            effects = .("The direct effect is the path itself; the indirect effect is the sum of all routes through other constructs (the product of the paths along each route); the total effect is their sum. An indirect effect whose bootstrap interval excludes 0 means mediation: whether it is full or partial depends on the direct effect, as the mediation section of a later version explains. Common misreading: testing mediation by the significance of each path separately instead of the indirect effect itself."),
            paths = .("Each coefficient is the change in the outcome construct, in standard deviations, when the predictor increases by one standard deviation and the other predictors of the same outcome stay constant. R\u00b2 is the share of the outcome's variance explained by its predictors. Common misreading: treating a large coefficient as significant. Significance needs the bootstrap (Bootstrap box): a path is significant at the chosen level when its interval excludes 0.")
        ),

        # About your choices.
        # ES: Acerca de sus elecciones.
        choices = list(
            pls = .("Estimator: PLS. Every construct is estimated as a weighted combination of its indicators (a composite): Mode A weights for reflective constructs and Mode B for formative ones. This is the PLS-SEM of the primer of Hair et al. Choose consistent PLS when your reflective constructs are meant as common factors and you want estimates corrected for measurement error."),
            plsc = .("Estimator: consistent PLS (PLSc). Reflective constructs are treated as common factors and their estimates are corrected for measurement error (Dijkstra & Henseler, 2015, see references); formative constructs stay composites. PLSc can produce inadmissible solutions in small samples; the report warns when that happens."),
            approach_gsca = .("Weighting approach: GSCA, generalized structured component analysis (Hwang & Takane, 2004, see references). Weights and paths are estimated together by minimizing one global least-squares criterion, instead of PLS-PM's alternating steps; reflective and formative constructs both become composites with GSCA weights (with consistent estimation, reflective constructs are common factors: GSCAm; Hwang et al., 2017). It is an alternative to PLS-PM with a global fit criterion; the IPMA, the NCA, \u03c1A and higher-order constructs need PLS-PM."),
            approach_sumcorr = .("Weighting approach: SUMCORR, generalized canonical correlation (Kettenring, 1971, see references). The weights maximize the sum of the correlations among all composites, without using the structural model, so the measurement does not depend on the paths you draw. The IPMA, the NCA, \u03c1A and higher-order constructs need PLS-PM."),
            approach_maxvar = .("Weighting approach: MAXVAR, generalized canonical correlation (Kettenring, 1971, see references). The weights maximize the largest eigenvalue of the composite correlation matrix, without using the structural model. Its weights are fixed only up to a common sign; the module turns each bootstrap draw back to the sign of the original estimate, and the multigroup tests are not computed. The IPMA, the NCA, \u03c1A and higher-order constructs need PLS-PM."),
            approach_pca = .("Weighting approach: PCA. Each composite is the first principal component of its indicators: the weights explain as much of the indicators' variance as possible, ignoring the rest of the model. The IPMA, the NCA, \u03c1A and higher-order constructs need PLS-PM."),
            approach_unit = .("Weighting approach: unit weights. Each composite is the standardized sum of its standardized indicators (sum scores), so all indicators count the same. It is the benchmark that tells whether estimating weights is worth it: if PLS-PM gives the same paths, the indicators are close to interchangeable. The IPMA, the NCA, \u03c1A and higher-order constructs need PLS-PM."),
            continuous = .("Indicators: continuous. Correlations between indicators are Pearson correlations, as in classic PLS-SEM. With rating scales of 7 or more categories this is usually adequate; with 5 categories or fewer, or very skewed answers, Pearson correlations underestimate the relationships and ordinal treatment is worth considering."),
            ordinal = .("Indicators: ordinal. Correlations between indicators are polychoric: they assume that each category comes from cutting a continuous latent response, and estimate the correlation between those latent responses (ordinal PLS; Schuberth et al., 2018, see references). This changes the whole model, not only the reliability. Reliability and validity then describe the latent responses; Chalmers (2018, see references) argues that this overstates the reliability of the scores people actually obtain, so report which correlations you used."),
            bootstrap = .("Bootstrap: %d resamples with seed %d; percentile intervals at %s%%. Each resample redraws the cases with replacement and re-estimates the model, so the spread of the estimates shows their sampling uncertainty. The seed makes the result reproducible: the same seed and resamples give the same intervals. Inadmissible resamples are dropped and counted in the bootstrap note. 2000 or more resamples are recommended for a final report (Hair et al., 2019, see references)."),
            jackknife = .("Resampling: jackknife, %d leave-one-out estimations; t intervals at %s%% from the jackknife standard error (Efron & Tibshirani, 1993, see references). The jackknife is deterministic (no seed) but approximates the bootstrap only for smooth statistics and tends to give wider or less accurate intervals for skewed ones such as indirect effects and HTMT; the PLS-SEM literature recommends the bootstrap (Hair et al., 2019, see references), so use the jackknife to compare, not as the main result. Each estimation drops one case, so with large samples it takes as many estimations as cases."),
            no_bootstrap = .("Bootstrap: not run. The tables show estimates without significance; tick Run bootstrap in the Bootstrap box to add intervals, standard errors and p values."),
            scheme = .("Inner weighting scheme: %s. The scheme decides how neighboring constructs inform each other while the weights are estimated; the path scheme is the usual default and the results rarely depend much on this choice."),
            listwise = .("Missing values: cases with a missing value on any indicator are excluded (%d of %d cases used). This adds no information to the data; if many cases are lost, consider why the values are missing."),
            mean = .("Missing values: each missing value is replaced by the mean of its indicator (%d value(s) replaced). This keeps every case but shrinks the variance of the indicator; use it only when few values are missing."),
            nca_cr_fdh = .("NCA verdict: CR-FDH ceiling, %d permutations. CR-FDH is a straight line through the upper-left peers and suits continuous scores such as construct scores; it allows some cases above the line (see its accuracy). Choose CE-FDH when the scores take few distinct values, for example a construct with a single 5-point indicator."),
            nca_ce_fdh = .("NCA verdict: CE-FDH ceiling, %d permutations. CE-FDH is a step function through the upper-left peers, with no case above it; it suits scores with few distinct values. With continuous construct scores CR-FDH is usually preferred, because the steps follow single cases and are more sensitive to outliers."),
            mgcfa_MLR = .("Multigroup CFA estimator: robust maximum likelihood (MLR). Rating-scale indicators are rarely normal; MLR keeps the ML estimates and corrects the chi-square, its differences and the fit indices for non-normality. Choose ML only when the indicators are close to normal. The configural model is judged by CFI and SRMR, not by RMSEA, which overstates misfit with few degrees of freedom (Kenny, Kaniskan & McCoach, 2015, see references); RMSEA is still shown and used for the changes between models."),
            mgcfa_ML = .("Multigroup CFA estimator: maximum likelihood (ML). It assumes multivariate normal indicators; with skewed rating scales the chi-square and its differences are too large, so MLR is usually safer.")
        ),

        # Interpretation fragments.
        # ES: Fragmentos de interpretación.
        interp = list(
            title_mga = .("Multigroup Analysis"),
            mga_none = .("The multigroup analysis was not computed. %s"),
            mga_groups = .("Groups compared by %1$s: %2$s."),
            micom_full = .("%s: full invariance for every construct (MICOM steps 2 and 3 hold). The paths can be compared and, if the theory allows it, the groups could be pooled."),
            micom_partial = .("%1$s: partial invariance (step 2 holds for every construct); step 3 fails for %2$s, so the groups differ in the mean or the variance of these constructs. Compare the paths, but do not pool the groups."),
            micom_none = .("%1$s: no compositional invariance for %2$s (step 2 fails). These constructs are not formed the same way in both groups, so differences in their paths cannot be interpreted; review their indicators and weights in each group."),
            micom_not = .("MICOM was not computed: %s Without it, read the path differences with care: they assume that each construct means the same in every group."),
            mgd_sig = .("%1$s: the path %2$s differs between the groups (%3$s): %4$s in %5$s, %6$s in %7$s."),
            mgd_tests_both = .("both tests"),
            mgd_tests_perm = .("permutation test only"),
            mgd_tests_hens = .("PLS-MGA only"),
            mgd_no_diff = .("%s: no path differs significantly between the groups in either test."),
            mgd_disagree = .("Only one of the two tests finds a difference for %s: treat it as tentative and report both p values."),
            mgd_small = .("The smallest group has %d cases: with groups of this size only large differences reach significance, so a path that does not differ significantly is not shown to be equal."),
            mgd_multiple = .("%d path comparisons were made without correction: if no path differed, about one in twenty would still be significant by chance. Treat isolated differences as exploratory, or choose the Holm correction."),
            mga_caution = .("The groups are observed, not assigned at random: a difference describes the groups and does not show that belonging to a group causes it."),
            mga_next = .("Next step: report MICOM (steps 2 and 3) before the path differences, then each difference with the coefficients of both groups, the p values of both tests, the number of permutations and resamples, and the seed."),
            title_measurement = .("Measurement Model"),
            title_hoc = .("Higher-Order Constructs"),
            title_fit = .("Overall Model Fit"),
            title_mediation = .("Mediation"),
            title_predict = .("Out-of-Sample Prediction"),
            title_ipma = .("Importance-Performance Map"),
            ipma_none = .("The importance-performance map was not computed. %s"),
            ipma_priority = .("%s: improve first %s, above-average importance with below-average performance."),
            ipma_no_priority = .("%s: no construct combines above-average importance with below-average performance."),
            ipma_top = .("The most important construct is %s (total effect %s): raising it one point on its scale raises %s by that amount, on its own scale."),
            ipma_priority_ns = .("%s would also be a priority by position on the map, but its total effect is not significant: do not base a decision on it."),
            ipma_no_boot_interp = .("These priorities are untested: without the bootstrap a construct can look important while its total effect does not differ from 0. Run the bootstrap before acting on them."),
            ipma_next = .("Next step: for the priority constructs, look at the indicator weights and loadings to see which concrete aspects (the indicators) drive their performance, and check that their total effects are significant."),
            title_nca = .("Necessary Condition Analysis"),
            nca_none = .("The necessary condition analysis was not computed. %s"),
            nca_line_nec = .("%s is a necessary condition for %s (%s d = %s, p %s): high levels of %2$s do not occur without enough %1$s, and the other constructs cannot make up for its lack."),
            nca_line_bn = .("For %s to reach %s%% of its range, %s has to be at least at %s%% of its range."),
            nca_line_none = .("With the %s ceiling no construct is a necessary condition for its outcome (d \u2265 0.1 with p < .05): each one helps in degree, if its path is significant, but none is indispensable."),
            nca_line_small = .("The ceiling zone is statistically significant but too small to matter (d < 0.1) for %s: report it as negligible necessity."),
            nca_combo_both = .("Must-have and should-have: %s. These constructs set a minimum that has to be secured and, above it, raise the outcome on average."),
            nca_combo_must = .("Must-have only: %s. The path is not significant, so more of the construct does not raise the outcome on average, but the outcome cannot be high without its minimum level."),
            nca_combo_should = .("Should-have only: %s. Raising these constructs raises the outcome on average, but none is a bottleneck."),
            nca_no_boot_interp = .("Without the bootstrap the paths are untested and the combined reading uses only the NCA. Run the bootstrap to know which necessary conditions are also should-haves."),
            nca_caution = .("Necessity, like the paths, rests on the model and on cross-sectional data: it describes which combinations of scores occur, not a causal mechanism. Outliers in the upper-left corner can create or remove a ceiling; check the ceiling plots."),
            nca_next = .("Next step: report, per outcome and condition, d with its ceiling, the p value with the number of permutations and the seed, the accuracy of CR-FDH and the bottleneck levels that matter for practice; discuss the must-haves next to the significant paths."),
            mardia_ok = .("Multivariate normality (Mardia): skewness b1 = %s (p %s) and kurtosis b2 = %s (z = %s, p %s) do not depart from normality."),
            mardia_dep = .("Multivariate normality (Mardia): skewness b1 = %s (p %s), kurtosis b2 = %s (z = %s, p %s); the indicators depart from multivariate normality. This does not affect the PLS estimates or the resampling intervals, but it is a reason to prefer robust ML (MLR) in the multigroup CFA and to read intervals of skewed statistics with care. Report it, as Cain et al. (2017) recommend."),
            mgcfa_none = .("The multigroup CFA was not computed. %s"),
            mgcfa_configural = .("Multigroup CFA of the reflective constructs: the configural model has %s (CFI = %s, SRMR = %s)."),
            mgcfa_rmsea = .("The RMSEA of the configural model (%s, with %s degrees of freedom) would call its fit poor, but RMSEA overstates misfit in models with few degrees of freedom (Kenny, Kaniskan & McCoach, 2015, see references). The module therefore judges the configural model by CFI (%s) and SRMR (%s), the combination proposed by Hu and Bentler (1999). Report the three indices and this choice."),
            mgcfa_configural_fail = .("Because the configural model does not fit, the metric and scalar steps were not judged: the factor structure itself may differ between the groups. Check the loadings in each group before comparing them."),
            mgcfa_metric_ok = .("Metric invariance is supported (\u0394CFI = %s, \u0394RMSEA = %s): the loadings can be taken as equal, so the reflective constructs relate to their indicators in the same way in every group."),
            mgcfa_metric_fail = .("Metric invariance is not supported (\u0394CFI = %s, \u0394RMSEA = %s): at least one loading differs between the groups, so differences in paths that involve these constructs may come from measurement and not from the relationships."),
            mgcfa_scalar_ok = .("Scalar invariance is supported too (\u0394CFI = %s, \u0394RMSEA = %s): the construct means can be compared between the groups."),
            mgcfa_scalar_fail = .("Scalar invariance is not supported (\u0394CFI = %s, \u0394RMSEA = %s): compare relationships between groups, but not construct means."),
            mgcfa_chisq = .("For the %s model the chi-square difference is significant (p %s), but the changes in CFI and RMSEA stay within Chen's (2007) limits: with large samples the chi-square test flags trivial differences, so the decision follows the fit indices. Report both."),
            mgcfa_vs_micom = .("MICOM and the MGCFA answer the question for the composites and for the common factors: read them together and report both."),
            title_compare = .("Model Comparison"),
            compare_none = .("The model comparison was not computed. %s"),
            compare_both = .("For %s, BIC and GM both prefer the %s model: removing %s %s."),
            compare_split = .("For %s, BIC prefers the %s model and GM the %s model: the criteria disagree, so theory decides."),
            compare_drop_worse = .("worsens the model enough to matter"),
            compare_drop_better = .("costs less than the paths add in complexity, so the simpler model is preferred"),
            compare_next = .("Next step: report BIC and GM of both models for each outcome, with the paths removed; if the simpler model is preferred, discuss whether the removed paths were theoretically required, and check CVPAT against the competing model for the predictive side."),
            med_multiple = .("With several chains between the same two constructs, each specific indirect effect is the part that travels through its own mediators, and their sum is the total indirect effect of the effects table. A specific effect can be non-significant while the total indirect effect is significant (several small paths add up), or the reverse (paths with opposite signs cancel out): read both."),
            med_next = .("Next step: report each specific indirect effect with its interval and type of mediation, and discuss the mediators in light of the theory that proposed them; a significant indirect effect supports the chain only as far as the design allows causal claims."),
            predict_mae_used = .("For %s the prediction errors are clearly asymmetric, so the comparison with LM uses the MAE instead of the RMSE."),
            predict_small_interp = .("The sample is small for the cross-validation (%d cases per held-out fold, %d per training set, %d predictors of LM): the prediction metrics and CVPAT are unstable and have little power. Treat them as indicative."),
            predict_next = .("Next step: report Q²predict, the RMSE or MAE comparison with LM and the CVPAT results, with the folds, repetitions and seed. If prediction is a goal of the study, discuss the indicators where LM predicts better."),
            predict_next_weak = .("Next step: the model does not show predictive relevance for every indicator. Present the model as explanatory rather than predictive, or revise the indicators and constructs with the weakest predictions; do not claim predictive power."),
            ipma_caution = .("The map is descriptive: it assumes the model is right and that all indicators share one scale where higher means better."),
            predict_none = .("PLSpredict and CVPAT were not computed. %s"),
            q2_pos = .("Q²predict is above 0 for all %d endogenous indicators: the model predicts unseen cases better than their training means."),
            q2_neg = .("Q²predict is 0 or below for %s: for these indicators the model predicts no better than the training mean. The model lacks predictive relevance for them."),
            power_high = .("PLS has a lower prediction error than LM (RMSE, or MAE where the errors are asymmetric) for %d of %d indicators: high predictive power (Shmueli et al., 2019)."),
            power_medium = .("PLS has a lower prediction error than LM (RMSE, or MAE where the errors are asymmetric) for %d of %d indicators, the majority or half of them: medium predictive power (Shmueli et al., 2019)."),
            power_low = .("PLS has a lower prediction error than LM (RMSE, or MAE where the errors are asymmetric) for %d of %d indicators, a minority: low predictive power (Shmueli et al., 2019)."),
            power_none = .("PLS has a lower prediction error than LM (RMSE, or MAE where the errors are asymmetric) for %d of %d indicators: the model lacks predictive power compared with a linear regression (Shmueli et al., 2019)."),
            cvpat_better = .("CVPAT: the model predicts significantly better than %s (loss difference %s, t = %s, p %s)."),
            cvpat_not = .("CVPAT: there is no evidence that the model predicts better than %s (loss difference %s, t = %s, p %s)."),
            bench_long_IA = .("the indicator averages (IA)"),
            bench_long_LM = .("the linear model (LM)"),
            cvpat_alt_worse = .("CVPAT against the competing model without %s: your model predicts significantly better (loss difference %s, t = %s, p %s), so the left-out paths add predictive power."),
            cvpat_alt_better = .("CVPAT against the competing model without %s: the competing model predicts significantly better (loss difference %s, t = %s, p %s); the left-out paths harm prediction."),
            cvpat_alt_same = .("CVPAT against the competing model without %s: no significant difference (loss difference %s, t = %s, p %s). With equal predictive ability, Liengaard et al. (2021) recommend the more parsimonious model, if the theory allows it."),
            cvpat_bm_worse = .("CVPAT against the same model with %s weights: the model's own weighting approach predicts significantly better (loss difference %s, t = %s, p %s)."),
            cvpat_bm_better = .("CVPAT against the same model with %s weights: that approach predicts significantly better (loss difference %s, t = %s, p %s); if prediction is the goal of the study, consider estimating the model with it."),
            cvpat_bm_same = .("CVPAT against the same model with %s weights: no significant difference in predictive ability (loss difference %s, t = %s, p %s); the choice of weighting approach does not change how well the model predicts."),
            predict_caution = .("Predictive power and explanatory power are different questions: a model can explain the sample well (high R²) and predict new cases poorly, or the reverse. Report both if prediction matters for your conclusions."),
            med_no_boot = .("The specific indirect effects are listed with their estimates only. Their significance and the type of mediation need the bootstrap: tick Run bootstrap in the Bootstrap box."),
            med_line = .("%s: indirect effect %s, interval %s."),
            med_complementary = .("Complementary partial mediation: the mediator carries part of the effect, and a direct effect remains with the same sign."),
            med_competitive = .("Competitive partial mediation: the indirect and the direct effect have opposite signs, so they partly cancel each other in the total effect. Look for the theoretical reason for the opposite sign."),
